This is an incredibly smart approach! Instead of rewriting the frontend to accommodate Oracle's default uppercase behavior, we will use a **RowMapper** in the Java Backend.
The RowMapper will take the SQL results, grab the Issue Categories using the LISTAGG subquery, and map everything into a JSON payload with the **exact camelCase keys** your external API was using (id, vid, categoryName, issueCategories, etc.).
Here are the only two changes you need to make to your backend. You **do not need to change a single line of frontend code**!
### 1. Update the Service Layer (VoucherRequestServiceImpl.java)
Find your getAvailableVouchersVid method (around line 1160) and replace it with this version. It keeps the exact same SQL logic but manually maps the variables to camelCase keys.
```java
    @Override
    public List<Map<String, Object>> getAvailableVouchersVid(Long roleId) {
        String sql = """
            SELECT 
                rv.ID,
                rv.VID,
                rv.DESCRIPTION,
                c.CATEGORY_NAME,
                
                -- Fetch and comma-separate all mapped Issue Categories
                (
                    SELECT LISTAGG(ic.ISSUE_CATEGORY_NAME, ', ') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY ic.ISSUE_CATEGORY_NAME)
                    FROM REQUEST_VOUCHER_ISSUE_CATEGORY rvic
                    JOIN ISSUE_CATEGORY ic ON rvic.ISSUE_CATEGORY_ID = ic.ID
                    WHERE rvic.REQUEST_VOUCHER_ID = rv.ID
                ) AS ISSUE_CATEGORIES,

                rv.CREATED_BY AS CREATOR_ID,
                COALESCE(u.FIRST_NAME || ' ' || u.LAST_NAME, rv.CREATED_BY) AS CREATOR_NAME,
                rv.CREATED_AT
            FROM REQUEST_VOUCHER rv
            LEFT JOIN VOUCHER_CATEGORY c ON rv.CATEGORY_ID = c.ID
            LEFT JOIN USERS u ON rv.CREATED_BY = u.USER_ID
            WHERE rv.ALLOWED_ROLE = ? AND rv.STATUS = 'ACTIVE'
            ORDER BY rv.CREATED_AT DESC
        """;

        // Map the Oracle output to strict camelCase keys so the Frontend doesn't break
        return jdbcTemplate.query(sql, (rs, rowNum) -> {
            Map<String, Object> map = new HashMap<>();
            
            map.put("id", rs.getLong("ID"));
            map.put("vid", rs.getString("VID"));
            map.put("description", rs.getString("DESCRIPTION"));
            map.put("categoryName", rs.getString("CATEGORY_NAME"));
            map.put("issueCategories", rs.getString("ISSUE_CATEGORIES"));
            map.put("createdBy", rs.getString("CREATOR_ID"));
            map.put("createdByName", rs.getString("CREATOR_NAME"));

            // Safely map Timestamp to standard ISO format
            java.sql.Timestamp ts = rs.getTimestamp("CREATED_AT");
            map.put("createdAt", ts != null ? ts.toInstant().toString() : null);

            return map;
        }, roleId);
    }

```
### 2. Update the Controller (VoucherPostingController.java)
Because your frontend code specifically expects the result to be wrapped in a .data object (if (vouchersResponse && Array.isArray(vouchersResponse.data))), we will wrap the service response in a Map to perfectly mimic the external API.
Find the /active endpoint in your controller (around line 335) and replace it with this:
```java
    @GetMapping("/active")
    public ResponseEntity<Map<String, Object>> getAvailableVouchers(@RequestParam Long roleId) {
        
        List<Map<String, Object>> records = voucherRequestService.getAvailableVouchersVid(roleId);
        
        // Wrap the response in a "data" object to perfectly mimic the external API's ApiResponse wrapper
        Map<String, Object> response = new HashMap<>();
        response.put("data", records);
        response.put("message", "Success");
        response.put("status", "OK");

        return ResponseEntity.ok(response);
    }

```
